    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## Claim\n\nAny skill writing into a long-lived knowledge layer should emit operations against a `KnowledgeUnit` (or equivalent) document model whose state requires epistemic context at write time.\n\n## Required standing\n\n- `author` (human or agent)\n- `producer` skill ref when agent-authored\n- `epistemicMode` (OBSERVATION, INFERENCE, RETRIEVAL, SYNTHESIS, SPECULATION)\n- optional `confidence`\n- `sources` and `derivedFrom`\n- `reviewStatus` and `scope`\n\n## Payoff\n\nThe vault becomes queryable: everything I asserted; exclude low-confidence agent units; all units derived from skill X. The human/agent boundary stays legible after the session ends.\n\n```graphql\ntype KnowledgeUnit {\n  id: ID!\n  claim: String!\n  author: Actor!\n  producer: SkillRef\n  epistemicMode: EpistemicMode!\n  confidence: Float\n  sources: [Source!]!\n  derivedFrom: [ID!]!\n  reviewStatus: ReviewStatus!\n  scope: Scope!\n  createdAt: DateTime!\n}\n```\n\n## Methodology grounding\nThe claims below live in the plugin's `data/methodology/` corpus; the relationship is stated per claim.\n\n- **[[agent self-memory should be architecturally separate from user knowledge systems]]** \u2014 BUILDS_ON. The methodology claim separates an agent's working preferences from the research graph on the grounds that self-knowledge need not pass the composability test. A typed KnowledgeUnit is the mechanism that makes the boundary enforceable rather than conventional: the schema is what refuses an untyped write into the long-lived layer.\n- **[[IBIS framework maps claim-based architecture to structured argumentation]]** \u2014 RELATES_TO. Both hold that a knowledge record's *kind* is load-bearing. IBIS types a contribution as issue, position or argument; a KnowledgeUnit types it by epistemic standing. Neither leaves the reader to infer status from prose.\n",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T10:03:23.304Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T10:03:23.305Z"
    }
  }
